**Design Opportunities

* Create a clear hero that immediately communicates:
  * Two-week work-experience programme
  * Six programme days
  * Approximate 10:00am–3:00pm hours
  * Main benefits
  * Clear next action

* Focus the website specifically on potential Movement to Work participants rather than competing employer and organisational audiences
* Create a visual two-week programme timeline so users can see exactly what happens during each stage

* Develop a dedicated skills section showing:
  * What skills participants will gain
  * How those skills are developed
  * Where those skills are useful in employment
  * How users can communicate those skills on their CV

* Introduce interactive quizzes to make employability content more engaging and memorable
* Create a transferable-skills quiz that helps users recognise skills they may already possess
* Consider a career or sector quiz for users who are unsure which type of employment may suit them
* Provide clear CV support with practical examples rather than only stating that CV support is available
* Explain how CVs can be tailored towards specific job opportunities
* Include structured interview preparation with example questions and guidance

* Provide clear guidance around using AI appropriately for:
  * CV assistance
  * Job applications
  * Employer research
  * Interview preparation

* Clearly explain the benefits users receive after completing the programme
* Highlight the certificate of participation and explain how it can strengthen a CV
* Include real participant outcomes or stories where these become available
* Show local employers and sectors relevant to Skelmersdale and surrounding areas
* Integrate travel information directly with local employment opportunities
* Help users understand whether an opportunity is realistically accessible through public transport
* Clearly explain what happens after completing Movement to Work

* Create a simple employment journey such as:
  * Discover
  * Prepare
  * Gain Experience
  * Build Skills
  * Complete
  * Find Opportunities

* Keep navigation intentionally small and focused rather than introducing large multi-level menus
* Use short, scannable sections to avoid the content overload identified across several competitors
* Maintain strong visual hierarchy across every page rather than only creating an engaging homepage
* Use relevant imagery to support content instead of adding imagery purely for decoration
* Avoid excessive animations and visual effects that could distract users or negatively affect performance
* Design mobile-first because some participants may primarily access the platform through a smartphone
* Ensure all important functionality works without requiring access to a desktop computer
* Use large, clearly labelled calls to action suitable for users with different levels of digital confidence
* Maintain consistent button styles and interaction states so users understand what is clickable
* Use plain English and avoid unnecessary employment terminology

* Provide clear FAQs covering:
  * Eligibility
  * Programme duration
  * Attendance
  * What participants will do
  * Travel
  * What to wear
  * Support available
  * What happens afterwards

* Allow users to access important information independently while still encouraging them to speak with their Work Coach
* Create clear progress indicators where users complete quizzes or employability activities
* Provide an obvious recommended starting point for users visiting the website for the first time
* Avoid dead-end journeys by always providing a relevant next action
* Ensure strong colour contrast, readable typography, keyboard accessibility and responsive layouts
* Keep the overall experience focused around one goal: helping users understand Movement to Work, prepare for the programme and progress towards employment
